  "preamble": "The open vowel [a] is rounded and backed to open-mid [ɔ]. In Bengali the Old Indo-Aryan and Prakrit inherent vowel a is pronounced [ɔ] (jala > জল /d͡ʒɔl/). In Central and Eastern Javanese, a in word-final open syllables and in an open penultimate before it became [ɔ] (Old Javanese bhāṣa > basa /bɔ.sɔ/), while Banyumasan keeps [a].",
  "phoneticEffects": "Rounding, Backing",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Old Javanese to Modern Javanese (Central and Eastern)",
      "languageFamily": "Malayo-Polynesian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old Javanese bhāṣa 'speech'",
          "to": "ꦧꦱ basa /bɔ.sɔ/ 'language'"
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Javanese Jawa 'Java'",
          "to": "Jawa /dʒɔ.wɔ/ (Standard, Central–Eastern)",
          "note": "Banyumasan keeps /dʒa.waʔ/."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old Javanese apa 'what'",
          "to": "ꦲꦥ apa [ɔpɔ] (Standard, Central, Eastern)",
          "note": "Banyumasan and Tengger keep [apa]. Synchronically [ɔ] is the realization of /a/ in word-final open syllables and in an open penultimate before such an [ɔ] (kaya [kɔjɔ] 'like'); elsewhere /a/ stays [a] (bali [bʰali] 'to go home')."
        }
      ]
    },
